00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.149
So let us start with
the virtual machines.

00:04:35.149 --> 00:04:36.440
They're pretty straightforward.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:37.730
They are virtual machines.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:40.480
You can choose one
from 64 processors.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:43.430
You can go all the way to 0.6
gigs of RAM all the way up

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:45.650
to 416.

00:04:45.650 --> 00:04:49.280
And basically, you're bound by
how many processors you use.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.930
You have to use a minimum
of 0.9 to a maximum

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.639
of 6.5 gigs per processor.

00:04:53.639 --> 00:04:55.430
Those of you that are
looking at the slides

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:58.380
carefully and doing math,
that doesn't add up.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.350
We have one or two
configurations that

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:03.660
have less than 0.9 gigs of RAM.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:04.990
You add disks to it.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:07.110
You can add simple
persistent storage.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.030
You can add SSDs.

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:10.800
You can add RAM disks.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.450
You can also--

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:16.020
I don't know if we announced
that yet, so I won't add that.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:19.180
We also have-- you can build off
of our preconfigured images, so

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:27.360
Debian, Ubuntu, Red Hat, other
flavors of Linux and Windows.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.570
You can customize those
images and save them,

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:33.330
or you can even upload
your own images.

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:36.931
And we like to say that our
VMs spin up in tens of seconds.

00:05:36.931 --> 00:05:37.930
What do we mean by that?

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:41.040
We mean that when you're
in your hotel room testing

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:44.180
the night before, they
spin up in 20 seconds.

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:47.140
When you're in front of a crowd
of people, they spin up in 40.

00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:48.270
I don't know how they know.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:50.020
I think it would be
in their best interest

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:51.730
to spin up quickly
in front of guests,

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:54.320
but they do what they do.

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:55.780
And you'll see that in a minute.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.637
Now, I just want a blank VM.

00:05:58.637 --> 00:05:59.720
I want to build it myself.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:00.219
That's fine.

00:06:00.219 --> 00:06:00.924
You get that.

00:06:00.924 --> 00:06:02.840
But a lot of times,
people want to fool around

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.685
