00:07:55.420 --> 00:07:57.760
So now we're going to
move on to containers.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:01.581
How many people here are fooling
around with containers already?

00:08:01.581 --> 00:08:02.080
OK.

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.451
How many are running
them in production?

00:08:05.451 --> 00:08:06.950
How many have said
the word "docker"

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:08.783
in a parking lot, and
a conference sprung up

00:08:08.783 --> 00:08:09.580
around you?

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:11.180
It happens.

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:11.810
So good.

00:08:11.810 --> 00:08:15.420
So containers is another
path for virtualization--

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.690
is an alternative
to virtualization.

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:21.830
Instead of carving
off a whole machine

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:26.150
and using all of the
resources dedicated to the VM,

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:27.980
you basically fool
all your hosts--

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:30.830
sorry, all your containers
into thinking they're

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:32.341
the only machine running.

00:08:32.341 --> 00:08:34.549
And so you can split up
processes and start things up

00:08:34.549 --> 00:08:36.919
really, really quickly
for those that aren't

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:39.440
as full on on containers yet.

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:42.260
That was a very quick
explanation for containers.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:45.230
So containers at Google--

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:47.810
first thing we talk about
is Container Engine,

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:49.670
which is Managed
Kubernetes, which

00:08:49.670 --> 00:08:52.550
leaves the obvious
question, kuber-what-es?

00:08:52.550 --> 00:08:56.060
Kubernetes is a container
orchestration system,

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:58.040
meaning if you want
to just spin up

00:08:58.040 --> 00:09:01.460
one container, dockers, great.

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:05.510
If you want to spin up
gigantic sets of interconnected

00:09:05.510 --> 00:09:08.030
containers that need
complex networking that

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:10.670
goes between them to
allocate resources

00:09:10.670 --> 00:09:13.760
and services between them,
it gets complicated and big.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:16.010
So here's a three-tier app.

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:19.310
I want to spin up a single
instance of a database

00:09:19.310 --> 00:09:20.520
that we would call a--

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:22.030
what would we call it?

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:25.460
I would call it a pet set, but
we call it something else now.

00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:26.600
But it's a pet.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:28.010
You just want one.

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:31.700
And then I've got the API layer,
which is multiple versions,

00:09:31.700 --> 00:09:34.550
multiple processes
running for the API layer,

00:09:34.550 --> 00:09:35.780
and a front layer.

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:37.820
All of these are wired together.

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:40.400
If one of them goes
down, it will--

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.842
because they said I want
four of the middle layer,

00:09:42.842 --> 00:09:45.300
it's going to make sure that
there are always four running.

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.180
So if one goes down,
it'll resurrect.

00:09:47.180 --> 00:09:51.324
And this allows you to
organize your containers

00:09:51.324 --> 00:09:52.490
into what we call workloads.

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:55.190
Now, a workload could
be a microservice,

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:57.697
which is a lot of what
containers have been known for.

00:09:57.697 --> 00:10:00.280
But it could also be things like
scheduled jobs or batch jobs,

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:02.960
anything where you need
to spin up large amounts

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:08.090
or small amounts of the same
process over and over again.

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.760
Containers and Kubernetes could
be a good solution for you.

00:10:10.760 --> 00:10:14.150
Now, this is running
on top of Kubernetes.

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:17.230
But from a systems
perspective, here's

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:18.949
what you have to do
to run Kubernetes.

00:10:18.949 --> 00:10:20.365
You have to build
up your machine.

00:10:20.365 --> 00:10:22.090
You have to pick
the OS, pick where

00:10:22.090 --> 00:10:24.970
you're going to run it on prem,
another cloud provider, or us.

00:10:24.970 --> 00:10:27.380
We would love to
have your business.

00:10:27.380 --> 00:10:29.440
And then you have to
install Kubernetes on them.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:30.730
And then once you
install Kubernetes,

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:32.010
you have to set up a network.

00:10:32.010 --> 00:10:34.900
You have to make sure that
network has DNS, which

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:36.550
helps with service discovery.

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:38.374
You then set up
logging and monitoring,

00:10:38.374 --> 00:10:39.790
because those
things are the sorts

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:41.980
of things we like to
know about our servers,

00:10:41.980 --> 00:10:43.600
that stuff is happening.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.400
Maybe you're not
cool with the fact

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:49.000
that they resurrect and go away,
the containers, so you want

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:50.770
to add some persistent storage.

00:10:50.770 --> 00:10:52.840
And now that you have your
Kubernetes cluster up,

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.520
you have to make
sure you maintain

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:56.890
the version of
Kubernetes and the OS,

00:10:56.890 --> 00:11:00.430
and make sure you patch the OS,
and handle all those updates

00:11:00.430 --> 00:11:03.730
and all of that maintenance.

00:11:03.730 --> 00:11:08.222
Or there's this little box
called Container Engine.

00:11:08.222 --> 00:11:09.930
And there's like a
little box right there

00:11:09.930 --> 00:11:12.620
that says Create a
Container Cluster for me.

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:14.351
And that's all you have to do.

00:11:14.351 --> 00:11:16.100
All of that thing, all
of that explanation

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:18.860
I did-- or you just
press that button.

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:21.470
So we like to say
that-- oh, I was

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:26.210
going to do a quick demo
of Container Engine.

00:11:26.210 --> 00:11:26.710
See?

00:11:26.710 --> 00:11:29.060
I have terminal up and ready.

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:29.560
All right.

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:33.810
So I'm going to run one command.

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:36.340
I'm going to show
you this visualizer.

00:11:36.340 --> 00:11:39.180
So this visualizer
is right now looking

00:11:39.180 --> 00:11:41.460
at the containers that are
running on my Kubernetes

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:43.210
cluster.

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:46.840
I'm going to run a make
command to get deploy.

00:11:46.840 --> 00:11:49.227
And we should see--

00:11:49.227 --> 00:11:51.310
assuming the network gods
have been-- there we go.

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:53.070
The network gods
have been appeased.

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:57.570
And so I am building multiple
systems at the same time.

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:01.020
I am building four
database servers

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:06.300
that power seven front
ends, two copies of Drupal,

00:12:06.300 --> 00:12:14.250
one copy of a custom app, a
Node.js app, WordPress, and one

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:16.050
other Zen developer server.

00:12:16.050 --> 00:12:17.970
So I just spun all of this up.

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:23.540
All these machines got their
networking, their back end,

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:24.660
and all spun from nothing.

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:27.480
And now the dark green ones
are going to get IP addresses.

00:12:27.480 --> 00:12:29.640
I don't think I have enough
time to dig into them.

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:31.710
But it's important to note
that those are public,

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:34.170
and the light green
ones-- the SQL servers--

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:36.450
we would like them
to not be public.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:37.620
And we can do that.

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:39.690
So in a very quick
way-- one of my apps

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:43.790
is up, so I'm just going to
click, see if it's working.

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:44.290
No.

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:47.800
All right.

00:12:47.800 --> 00:12:49.140
Ditch.

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:50.810
Don't have enough time.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:52.810
I think something is weird
in the browser there.

00:12:52.810 --> 00:12:53.309
OK.

00:12:53.309 --> 00:12:57.500
So that puts us here.

00:12:57.500 --> 00:12:59.420
So that's just a
quick launching up

00:12:59.420 --> 00:13:01.732
a whole bunch of different
workloads on Kubernetes.

00:13:01.732 --> 00:13:02.440
I can do it fast.

00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:04.190
I can do it scripted.

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:06.170
Kubernetes is what
we call-- sorry.

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:09.920
Container Engine is managed
Kubernetes or containers

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:11.150
as a service.

00:13:11.150 --> 00:13:13.910
We have smart default set
for monitoring and logging,

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:15.800
DNS and service discovery.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:17.850
And we can also auto scale.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:20.980
So you can set up your
Kubernetes cluster on GKE

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:23.330
to basically-- as
your load goes up,

00:13:23.330 --> 00:13:25.970
spin up more hardware resources
in order to handle it,

00:13:25.970 --> 00:13:28.950
or spin down, as
the case may be.

00:13:28.950 --> 00:13:30.830
And so that's container engine.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:34.220
Now, another way we have
of dealing with containers

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:37.250
is something called app
engine flexible environment.

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:39.290
Now, App Engine-- when
we talk about app engine,

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:42.680
we talk about it usually
in terms of serverless.

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:44.450
Because it's a
platform as a service.

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:48.200
You don't ever
really interact with

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:49.550
the underlying infrastructure.

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:52.122
But under the covers,
App Engine flexible--

00:13:52.122 --> 00:13:54.080
on App Engine standard,
but App Engine flexible

00:13:54.080 --> 00:13:56.540
is running Docker.

00:13:56.540 --> 00:14:00.380
So you can flip a bit and
say runtime equals custom.

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:02.780
And then you can put whatever
docker file you want,

00:14:02.780 --> 00:14:05.300
and we will run it
as an App Engine app.

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:07.190
Now, what that buys
you is connecting

00:14:07.190 --> 00:14:08.810
to a lot of our resources.

00:14:08.810 --> 00:14:11.360
And you get auto scaling
out of the box from 1

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:13.580
to however big you need to grow.

00:14:13.580 --> 00:14:15.560
And then you get one
endpoint for service.

00:14:15.560 --> 00:14:19.220
So a lot of the work of setting
up a service kind of goes out.

00:14:19.220 --> 00:14:21.350
You don't have to do it.

00:14:21.350 --> 00:14:24.740
So that is App Engine
flexible environment.

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:28.670
There are two helpers to
help you deal with containers

00:14:28.670 --> 00:14:29.910
in Google Cloud platform.

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:31.980
We have Container Registry
and Container Builder.

00:14:31.980 --> 00:14:35.780
Container Registry is a storage
repository for Docker images.

00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:39.429
So you build your images, you
push them up to the registry,

00:14:39.429 --> 00:14:40.970
and then they're
behind our security,

00:14:40.970 --> 00:14:45.020
so you can lock them down with
your Google Cloud account.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:49.670
And you can pull them from
Container Engine or App Engine

00:14:49.670 --> 00:14:53.094
Flexible or a Docker client
running on your own machine.

00:14:53.094 --> 00:14:55.010
And in fact, what I
usually do is I push stuff

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:57.050
up the container registry.

00:14:57.050 --> 00:15:00.470
I will be in production
on Kubernetes.

00:15:00.470 --> 00:15:03.380
But in development, I
will develop on Docker.

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:06.890
So I can use the same image in
the same builds in both places.

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:11.112
Container Builder is brand
new, as in Monday it came out.

00:15:11.112 --> 00:15:13.070
It is a service-- if
you're doing a lot of work

00:15:13.070 --> 00:15:14.750
on containers, you
reach a point where

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:18.020
you need to build
infrastructure to make sure

00:15:18.020 --> 00:15:20.660
that your builds are
consistent and you're

00:15:20.660 --> 00:15:23.510
going through whatever
continuous integration

00:15:23.510 --> 00:15:25.340
tools you have.

00:15:25.340 --> 00:15:27.884
Container Builder will
just do that for you.

00:15:27.884 --> 00:15:29.300
And it can integrate
with other CI

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:31.520
systems and other repositories.

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:34.010
Or you will just
build it and store it

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:36.950
for you on Container
Registry and speed up

00:15:36.950 --> 00:15:37.760
that whole process.

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:42.045
That's containers.

00:15:42.045 --> 00:15:44.760
I'll move on to our
friends serverless.

00:15:44.760 --> 00:15:47.750
Serverless-- we have our friend
app engineer flexible runtime.

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:50.370
Now, I, said it was a container
technology, and it is.

00:15:50.370 --> 00:15:52.320
But if you don't
flip the switch,

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:54.000
you're going to use
one of our run times

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:55.860
instead of your own Docker file.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:58.020
And the runtime under the
covers is a Docker file

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:00.960
that we built
specifically for this.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:02.640
We have set languages
built already,

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:04.110
and we're working
on adding more.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:09.210
But it's Java 8, Python 2, 7,
and 3.5, Node.js, Ruby, PHP,

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:10.830
and Go.

00:16:10.830 --> 00:16:13.260
Version numbers are a
little muddled right now,

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:16.290
because I can't remember
if we've released the thing

00:16:16.290 --> 00:16:18.070
or not.

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:22.169
So the big
differentiator here is

00:16:22.169 --> 00:16:24.210
that under the covers,
like I said, it is Docker.

00:16:24.210 --> 00:16:28.500
So you can add extra
things to our Docker file.

00:16:28.500 --> 00:16:32.220
So you can add extra software,
add extra configuration,

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:36.400
and really open up the
capabilities of your machine--

00:16:36.400 --> 00:16:37.740
I'm sorry-- your application.

00:16:37.740 --> 00:16:40.735
As opposed to app
engine standard runtime,

00:16:40.735 --> 00:16:43.110
which is much more locked down
in terms of what languages

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:44.130
you can use.

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:48.150
Python 2.7, Java 7, PHP 5.5, Go.

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:52.190
1.6 or 8-- can't
remember where it's at--

00:16:52.190 --> 00:16:54.420
the abilities are
much more constrained.

00:16:54.420 --> 00:16:58.470
For example, PHP you can extend
in C if you need to, normally.

00:16:58.470 --> 00:17:00.510
But on App Engine Standard,
you can't do that.

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:03.030
You can do that on flex.

00:17:03.030 --> 00:17:06.390
But on standard, you
can't add extra code.

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:07.940
You can add more PHP.

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:10.440
As long as your extensions
are in PHP, it'll work fine.

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:13.740
But once you try to dip
out of PHP, it won't work--

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:15.812
or Java, or Python, or Go.

00:17:15.812 --> 00:17:17.520
Now, what do you get
for this constraint?

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:20.670
You get incredibly
rapid scaling.

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:22.420
And I'm going to show
that off in a second

00:17:22.420 --> 00:17:27.280
to make it clear why the
scaling really is rapid

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:29.080
and why you might use it.

00:17:29.080 --> 00:17:31.890
The other thing is it
scales down to zero.

00:17:31.890 --> 00:17:33.970
Flex will scale
down to one instance

00:17:33.970 --> 00:17:36.610
and have one instance responding
to requests all the time.

00:17:36.610 --> 00:17:39.430
App Engine standard
will scale down to zero

00:17:39.430 --> 00:17:43.720
and will not charge
you while it's at zero,

00:17:43.720 --> 00:17:46.560
which is a nice feature.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:48.890
Finally, we have cloud
functions, which we're really

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:50.056
excited to be talking about.

00:17:50.056 --> 00:17:51.800
There are demos
out in the showcase

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:54.020
area that talk about this.

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:56.870
App Engine-- you're
abstracted to an app,

00:17:56.870 --> 00:17:58.730
and you move an app
from place to place.

00:17:58.730 --> 00:18:01.929
But the unit of abstraction
with functions is a function.

00:18:01.929 --> 00:18:02.970
Why does that make sense?

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:05.810
Well, let's say I've got a
mobile app or some other thing,

00:18:05.810 --> 00:18:07.537
and all the logic
is somewhere else.

00:18:07.537 --> 00:18:08.870
And I just need to do one thing.

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:11.460
This is the halcyon,
perfect example of this.

00:18:11.460 --> 00:18:14.420
I just want to send
mail from my mobile app.

00:18:14.420 --> 00:18:17.750
You could write some code,
build an application,

00:18:17.750 --> 00:18:21.650
set up routing so that your
API is easily called and locked

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:24.770
behind security, and everything
so that app can send a mail.

00:18:24.770 --> 00:18:26.510
You could build an
application for it.

00:18:26.510 --> 00:18:28.070
Or with cloud
functions, you just

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:30.350
write the one function
that sends the mail,

00:18:30.350 --> 00:18:31.970
and it'll create
an endpoint for you

00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:33.860
so you can attach to
your app, and so you

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:36.140
don't have to build an
entire application just

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:38.220
to serve up one function.

00:18:38.220 --> 00:18:40.440
So that is the main
differentiator there.

00:18:40.440 --> 00:18:45.600
And it is JavaScript,
Node.js only at the moment.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:50.630
So I promised a demo of App
Engine in action taking load.

00:18:50.630 --> 00:18:55.300
So here's my Kubernetes app.

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:57.650
It decided to load.

00:18:57.650 --> 00:19:02.414
So I have here a set of 10
VMs, Compute Engine VMs.

00:19:02.414 --> 00:19:03.830
And they're going
to hit that URL.

00:19:03.830 --> 00:19:05.060
Please-- you can hit the URL.

00:19:05.060 --> 00:19:05.851
Don't hit that URL.

00:19:05.851 --> 00:19:07.070
No, no, don't.

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:08.990
Because you'll break the whole--

00:19:08.990 --> 00:19:13.280
from cold, we're going to
see how App Engine scales.

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:16.070
So you can hit it after
I'm done with the demo

00:19:16.070 --> 00:19:18.440
if that's your thing.

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:22.800
So I'm going to go ahead
and run one make command.

00:19:22.800 --> 00:19:24.330
And I'll explain what I'm doing.

00:19:24.330 --> 00:19:26.040
I'm instructing
each one of my VMs

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:34.170
to send 1,000 hits at that URL,
and to do it concurrently at 50

00:19:34.170 --> 00:19:37.720
apiece, so a 500 QPS query.

00:19:37.720 --> 00:19:39.120
So I'm going to run that.

00:19:39.120 --> 00:19:40.578
I'm going to switch
back over here.

00:19:40.578 --> 00:19:42.030
Someone hit the URL.

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:43.510
Someone did it.

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:44.670
That's all right.

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:46.020
You'll still see some magic.

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:47.520
So in a moment,
they're going to see

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:50.370
as soon as Compute
Engine gets the traffic

00:19:50.370 --> 00:19:52.350
and starts sending it,
they start bouncing.

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:53.850
And that is App
Engine spinning up

00:19:53.850 --> 00:19:56.250
instances of my
application in order

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:57.954
to handle the load
that's coming in.

00:19:57.954 --> 00:19:59.370
And basically,
what will happen is

00:19:59.370 --> 00:20:01.036
it can handle-- each
instance can handle

00:20:01.036 --> 00:20:03.194
a certain amount of traffic
at once and then say,

00:20:03.194 --> 00:20:04.110
hey, I'm a little peg.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.318
And we'll start spinning up
more instance to take it.

00:20:06.318 --> 00:20:09.030
That's how quickly we
spin up instances in order

00:20:09.030 --> 00:20:10.417
to deal with your traffic.

00:20:10.417 --> 00:20:12.000
And that's why the
whole scale to zero

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:16.350
is awesome, because it quickly
responds when you have traffic.

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:18.210
But then when we
don't have traffic,

00:20:18.210 --> 00:20:22.920
it starts spinning down
instances, and three of you.

00:20:22.920 --> 00:20:24.780
J'accuse three of you--

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:25.310
four.

00:20:25.310 --> 00:20:25.630
All right.

00:20:25.630 --> 00:20:26.430
Well, now I don't care.

00:20:26.430 --> 00:20:28.221
Now you can just ping
it away all you want.

00:20:28.221 --> 00:20:30.730
And you can see the
numbers go up in real time.

00:20:30.730 --> 00:20:32.220
Yeah, you're really doing that.

00:20:32.220 --> 00:20:35.586
You're really hitting the URL.

00:20:35.586 --> 00:20:36.840
Oh, all right.

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:38.500
I've challenged people there.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:39.660
They're feeling feisty.

00:20:39.660 --> 00:20:43.320
So that's just a quick demo
of what App Engine can do.

00:20:43.320 --> 00:20:47.730
And with that, we get to
the end of that section.

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:50.310
We are done all our
computing technology.

00:20:50.310 --> 00:20:53.100
You should have a good idea
of where to do containers,

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:56.020
where to do serverless,
what the difference

00:20:56.020 --> 00:20:59.454
between the serverless are, and
that our VMs are pretty quick.

00:20:59.454 --> 00:21:01.120
So with that, I'm
going to change gears.

00:21:01.120 --> 00:21:03.830
And this is going to be
more explaining things.

00:21:03.830 --> 00:21:06.180
The networking is
incredibly complex,

00:21:06.180 --> 00:21:08.592
but you can't really
get into it in this,

00:21:08.592 --> 00:21:10.050
so I'm just going
to tell you where

00:21:10.050 --> 00:21:12.070
to look for certain things.

00:21:12.070 --> 00:21:14.670
So networking tends to be
about connections, security,

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:16.440
and availability.

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:18.290
People are looking
to connect us.

00:21:18.290 --> 00:21:20.790
First thing is we have something
called the virtual network,

00:21:20.790 --> 00:21:22.206
Cloud Virtual
Network, that allows

00:21:22.206 --> 00:21:25.230
you to build your own private
networks for your project

00:21:25.230 --> 00:21:28.367
and set up custom routes
to non-Google locations.

00:21:28.367 --> 00:21:30.450
We also have this thing
called Cloud Interconnect,

00:21:30.450 --> 00:21:31.366
which is kind of cool.

00:21:31.366 --> 00:21:33.951
You directly peer to Google.

00:21:33.951 --> 00:21:35.700
If you are doing a lot
of business with us

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:37.650
and you're transferring a lot
of data back from your network

00:21:37.650 --> 00:21:39.270
to our network, it
may be something

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:42.390
that would be cost
effective for you.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:45.090
Security from a
networking perspective--

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:47.220
two major ways we look at this--

00:21:47.220 --> 00:21:49.740
VPN, which is gateway
to gateway only for us.

00:21:49.740 --> 00:21:52.794
So this isn't for equipping
road warriors with a connection

00:21:52.794 --> 00:21:53.460
to your network.

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:58.170
This is for connecting
two or more networks

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.150
via secure tunnel.

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.650
And you can use any
IPsec complaint gateway.

00:22:02.650 --> 00:22:04.800
But the idea here is
you have machines at us,

00:22:04.800 --> 00:22:07.920
machines on prem, machines
at Amazon or Azure

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:09.690
that you want to
connect together.

00:22:09.690 --> 00:22:11.820
You can use this to do it.

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:13.640
Firewall-- it's a firewall.

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:14.850
You set up rules.

00:22:14.850 --> 00:22:15.990
It's nothing too crazy.

00:22:15.990 --> 00:22:16.950
But the reason why
I bring it here

00:22:16.950 --> 00:22:18.980
is because this is about
discovering things.

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:20.730
When I first started
using Compute Engine,

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:22.646
one of the problems I
had was figuring out how

00:22:22.646 --> 00:22:24.690
the hell do I open up a port.

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:28.290
I just want to open up some
random port, as one does.

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:30.300
And you have to basically
go through networking

00:22:30.300 --> 00:22:32.924
to find the firewall settings to
create firewall rules that you

00:22:32.924 --> 00:22:34.830
can then apply in
Compute Engine.

00:22:34.830 --> 00:22:37.080
But it is maybe less than
intuitive the first time

00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:39.413
you come to it, so I wanted
to make sure to call it out.

00:22:39.413 --> 00:22:42.360
Look in the Networking
section of our app--

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:46.620
of our console is
what we call it--

00:22:46.620 --> 00:22:49.775
to find these things.

00:22:49.775 --> 00:22:51.900
At some point, someone
says, well, I want something

00:22:51.900 --> 00:22:54.450
that you don't have.

00:22:54.450 --> 00:22:55.870
What can I do?

00:22:55.870 --> 00:22:58.500
Well, you can, in
Compute Engine,

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:00.230
build anything you want by hand.

00:23:00.230 --> 00:23:02.490
And we actually have,
in Cloud Launcher,

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:06.210
VM setups for Baracuda, Brocade,
CloudFlare, and a whole bunch

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:06.810
of others.

00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:11.430
I clearly went
alphabetical in my list.

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:14.010
And you can build these,
and you can manage them.

00:23:14.010 --> 00:23:18.220
We don't manage these for you,
but you absolutely can do that.

00:23:18.220 --> 00:23:20.680
Now, talking about
availability--

00:23:20.680 --> 00:23:23.311
we have cloud CDN,
which you activate--

00:23:23.311 --> 00:23:25.810
the interface for it is actually
through our load balancers,

00:23:25.810 --> 00:23:27.310
which I'll talk
about in a Second.

00:23:27.310 --> 00:23:30.700
And it basically uses
HTTP headers in order

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:33.190
to determine cacheability.

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:37.690
You can do ed caching, and
invalidation, and pre-loading,

00:23:37.690 --> 00:23:39.617
and all sorts of things
you do with content.

00:23:39.617 --> 00:23:41.200
If you want to do
more complex things,

00:23:41.200 --> 00:23:44.820
this is not the
solution for you.

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:47.230
This is not meant to be
a comprehensive solution.

00:23:47.230 --> 00:23:49.720
People want this as part
of a basic cloud package.

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:50.830
We offer it.

00:23:50.830 --> 00:23:52.660
If you need more
complex things, we

00:23:52.660 --> 00:23:55.420
have partners that are
more than happy to talk

00:23:55.420 --> 00:23:58.030
to you about doing more
complex things with caching

00:23:58.030 --> 00:24:01.140
with us and them.

00:24:01.140 --> 00:24:03.090
So next is DNS.

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:05.790
This is interesting in that
it is the only thing I've ever

00:24:05.790 --> 00:24:08.040
seen where we've promise 100%--

00:24:08.040 --> 00:24:10.830
at Google that we've
promised 100% availability.

00:24:10.830 --> 00:24:12.480
Our SLA is 100% availability.

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:13.539
And I had to double--

00:24:13.539 --> 00:24:14.830
they didn't round up somewhere?

00:24:14.830 --> 00:24:15.330
No.

00:24:15.330 --> 00:24:17.340
It's 100% availability
for our DNS servers,

00:24:17.340 --> 00:24:19.020
so something to keep in mind.

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:20.070
That's interesting.

00:24:20.070 --> 00:24:22.320
And then finally, our load
balancers-- a lot of people

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:25.950
talk about load balancers
in terms of L4 versus L7.

00:24:25.950 --> 00:24:27.510
We don't.

00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:30.630
We talk about HTTP and
SSL load balancers, which

00:24:30.630 --> 00:24:32.610
do what you'd imagine they do.

00:24:32.610 --> 00:24:35.970
Internal, it's for load
balancing traffic that

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:37.200
would not leave our network.

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:39.630
So a lot of times, people
want load balancing

00:24:39.630 --> 00:24:40.620
of internal services.

00:24:40.620 --> 00:24:41.594
We can now do that.

00:24:41.594 --> 00:24:43.260
And then finally,
network load balancing

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:46.170
is for balancing more
than just HTTP traffic.

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:51.700
My first auctioneer
section is done,

00:24:51.700 --> 00:24:54.355
so now I'm going to move
on to storage, where

00:24:54.355 --> 00:24:56.080
I'll go a little
bit deeper and talk

00:24:56.080 --> 00:25:00.520
about why you would use one
versus the other and whatnot.

00:25:00.520 --> 00:25:04.720
So with storage, we tend to be
thinking along three lines--

00:25:04.720 --> 00:25:07.450
files.

00:25:07.450 --> 00:25:09.535
I was heavily encouraged
to use the word "blob."

00:25:09.535 --> 00:25:12.160
But as an adult human, I really
don't want to get up on a stage

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:13.690
and say the word "blob"
over and over again.

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:14.939
But I did, so they tricked me.

00:25:14.939 --> 00:25:17.800
So [SIGH] they won.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:20.860
But think of this in terms
of documents, of images,

00:25:20.860 --> 00:25:22.990
of videos, of audio files.

00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:25.120
They're files.

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:26.507
You use the word "unstructured."

00:25:26.507 --> 00:25:27.340
They have structure.

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:31.597
They're just not related
to each other that much.

00:25:31.597 --> 00:25:33.430
As opposed to databases,
where you have data

00:25:33.430 --> 00:25:34.270
that all looks the same.

00:25:34.270 --> 00:25:36.145
It's all telling the
same story, and it's all

00:25:36.145 --> 00:25:39.700
supposed to be lined up
and easy to aggregate

00:25:39.700 --> 00:25:42.310
and talk to it together.

00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:43.494
I also liked--

00:25:43.494 --> 00:25:45.160
I also think of this
as application data

00:25:45.160 --> 00:25:50.220
as opposed to files,
but semantics vary.

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:53.290
And finally, big data,
which under the covers

00:25:53.290 --> 00:25:55.150
is really just one
of the other two

00:25:55.150 --> 00:25:58.210
that's gotten so big that
to get insight out of it,

00:25:58.210 --> 00:26:02.070
you need to run the
special processes on it.

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:05.640
So big data is just
any one of these things

00:26:05.640 --> 00:26:08.290
that's grown out of control.

00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:11.000
So we'll start
with file storage.

00:26:11.000 --> 00:26:11.950
You're storing files.

00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:16.620
If you are used to some other
provider that calls it S3,

00:26:16.620 --> 00:26:19.645
this is the equivalent to that.

00:26:19.645 --> 00:26:21.770
They get a little touchy
about us and other brands,

00:26:21.770 --> 00:26:24.550
and I'm being recorded,
so I'm nervous.

00:26:24.550 --> 00:26:27.910
So cloud storage is
the S3 equivalent.

00:26:27.910 --> 00:26:30.240
We have one set of
interfaces so you deal only

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:31.267
with cloud storage.

00:26:31.267 --> 00:26:32.100
You create a bucket.

00:26:32.100 --> 00:26:33.570
And the way to deal
with that bucket

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:35.319
is the same no matter
what type of storage

00:26:35.319 --> 00:26:37.590
you're talking about.

00:26:37.590 --> 00:26:40.320
So when you create
a bucket, that's it.

00:26:40.320 --> 00:26:42.960
But we have several types--
multi regional, nearline,

00:26:42.960 --> 00:26:43.860
and coldline.

00:26:43.860 --> 00:26:47.840
I will describe why you'd want
to use one versus the other.

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:50.710
So thinking about file,
we have three areas

00:26:50.710 --> 00:26:52.450
that we look at
it-- public content,

00:26:52.450 --> 00:26:54.741
content that we're going to
share with the world, share

00:26:54.741 --> 00:26:55.690
with our customers.

00:26:55.690 --> 00:26:59.710
Process data-- we have data
that we need to run analysis on.

00:26:59.710 --> 00:27:01.100
It doesn't need to be global.

00:27:01.100 --> 00:27:02.860
I don't need to
share it with people.

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:04.510
I want it to be
close to the servers,

00:27:04.510 --> 00:27:06.134
because I'm going to
be doing the work.

00:27:06.134 --> 00:27:07.020
So regional is fine.

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:09.820
It's in the same data center,
the same data location.

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:13.510
And then long-term
storage is for stuff that,

00:27:13.510 --> 00:27:17.350
at the end of the day, we
really hope we never see again--

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:22.270
so things like backups, and
disaster recovery-related data,

00:27:22.270 --> 00:27:24.010
and compliance.

00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:25.650
And people hate compliance.

00:27:25.650 --> 00:27:28.150
Because after-- what
did you do shortly

00:27:28.150 --> 00:27:30.600
before you pulled in a whole
bunch of compliance data?

00:27:30.600 --> 00:27:33.100
You had a conversation
with a lawyer.

00:27:33.100 --> 00:27:36.359
So we don't want to
touch this stuff.

00:27:36.359 --> 00:27:38.650
We need to keep it, but we
don't ever want to touch it.

00:27:38.650 --> 00:27:41.210
So we want it to be
as cheap as possible.

00:27:41.210 --> 00:27:44.750
So putting that together,
we'll start at the top

00:27:44.750 --> 00:27:46.650
and go to the bottom,
name the type.

00:27:46.650 --> 00:27:48.500
We have public storage.

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:50.630
For us, it's 99.9--

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:52.870
three nines plus a
five availability.

00:27:52.870 --> 00:27:56.690
Geo-redundant-- this is good
for world access content, things

00:27:56.690 --> 00:28:01.550
like websites, streaming
video, streaming audio, gaming,

00:28:01.550 --> 00:28:02.360
mobile.

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:07.370
And it's 2.6 cents per
gigabyte per month.

00:28:07.370 --> 00:28:09.937
And this we call multi-region.

00:28:09.937 --> 00:28:12.020
For process data, we have
a little less available.

00:28:12.020 --> 00:28:15.426
It makes sense, because
it's not geo-redundant.

00:28:15.426 --> 00:28:16.800
And it's accessed
by your system,

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:19.700
so you need to run big
data work, big data jobs.

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:22.160
You want this data to
only live in one place

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:25.490
and be pulled in
by a set of servers

00:28:25.490 --> 00:28:26.960
in one geographic location.

00:28:26.960 --> 00:28:28.477
We do that for you.

00:28:28.477 --> 00:28:29.060
It is cheaper.

00:28:29.060 --> 00:28:31.010
It's $0.02 per
gigabyte per month.

00:28:31.010 --> 00:28:32.540
We call it regional.

00:28:32.540 --> 00:28:35.990
Then we have two options
in long-term storage.

00:28:35.990 --> 00:28:37.090
It's not as available.

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:40.520
Your time to first bite
we measure in seconds

00:28:40.520 --> 00:28:41.780
instead of milliseconds.

00:28:41.780 --> 00:28:44.030
But you'll still get good
throughput pulling the stuff

00:28:44.030 --> 00:28:45.260
down.

00:28:45.260 --> 00:28:47.741
You have to keep in us
for 30 days minimal.

00:28:47.741 --> 00:28:49.490
Now, that doesn't mean
you have to keep it

00:28:49.490 --> 00:28:50.821
on us for 30 days minimal.

00:28:50.821 --> 00:28:52.320
If you want to
delete it, it's fine.

00:28:52.320 --> 00:28:53.210
It deletes.

00:28:53.210 --> 00:28:53.865
It's fine.

00:28:53.865 --> 00:28:55.490
We're still going to
charge you for it.

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:58.310
That's the 30-day minimum.

00:28:58.310 --> 00:29:02.210
And it's for backup and maybe
long-term, longtail multimedia.

00:29:02.210 --> 00:29:05.780
You have a video file that's
looked at every six months.

00:29:05.780 --> 00:29:07.370
Putting it in cold
storage and making

00:29:07.370 --> 00:29:10.372
it public from
cold storage, it's

00:29:10.372 --> 00:29:12.080
fine, because it's
stored really cheaply,

00:29:12.080 --> 00:29:14.750
because it's a video
file, so it's bigger.

00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:17.090
Storage cost is $0.01
per gigabyte per month.

00:29:17.090 --> 00:29:20.000
But if you retrieve
it, we're going

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:23.270
to charge a retrieval cost
for it that goes on top

00:29:23.270 --> 00:29:26.270
and is $0.01 per
gigabyte per month.

00:29:26.270 --> 00:29:29.072
And our last option
is cold line,

00:29:29.072 --> 00:29:30.530
which is just as
available, but you

00:29:30.530 --> 00:29:32.660
have to store it for 90
days instead of 30 days.

00:29:32.660 --> 00:29:34.880
And again, delete it, it's fine.

00:29:34.880 --> 00:29:36.890
It's just about the charging.

00:29:36.890 --> 00:29:41.630
And it's for disaster recovery
and for compliance data.

00:29:41.630 --> 00:29:43.250
And it is the
cheapest storage we

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:48.200
have at $0.07 per
gigabyte per month.

00:29:48.200 --> 00:29:50.090
But retrieving it
is slightly-- we're

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:53.060
incentivizing you to not
pull it out too often.

00:29:53.060 --> 00:29:55.080
It's $0.05 per
gigabyte per month.

00:29:55.080 --> 00:29:57.680
That's coldline.

00:29:57.680 --> 00:29:59.410
So we move on to
application data,

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:04.230
and we move on to one of the
giant fights of our day--

00:30:04.230 --> 00:30:07.400
SQL versus no SQL.

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:13.130
We could have a whole session, a
whole conference, a whole fleet

00:30:13.130 --> 00:30:17.090
of events that just hold
conferences and sell books

00:30:17.090 --> 00:30:20.420
and online seminars
about this topic.

00:30:20.420 --> 00:30:22.430
So I'm not choosing a fight.

00:30:22.430 --> 00:30:25.610
I'm saying we have
NoSQL and we have SQL.

00:30:25.610 --> 00:30:26.114
It's fine.

00:30:26.114 --> 00:30:27.530
We have both, and
we welcome both,

00:30:27.530 --> 00:30:28.780
even if we have our favorites.

00:30:28.780 --> 00:30:31.800
I like SQL.

00:30:31.800 --> 00:30:34.110
But we have good
options on both.

00:30:34.110 --> 00:30:36.900
So I'm going to talk
first about SQL.

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:39.780
We have Cloud SQL, which is
a traditional SQL Server.

00:30:39.780 --> 00:30:44.190
It is based on our VM, so it is
vertically scalable to the size

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:46.470
of our VMs.

00:30:46.470 --> 00:30:48.720
But you don't ever
actually-- there's

00:30:48.720 --> 00:30:51.420
no-- you won't see the VM in
your list of Compute Engine

00:30:51.420 --> 00:30:52.470
instances.

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:56.199
We manage the OS
entirely for you.

00:30:56.199 --> 00:30:57.990
And we do things like
managing the backups,

00:30:57.990 --> 00:31:00.030
and we make setting
up replication sets

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:03.000
and opening up network
connections pretty easy.

00:31:03.000 --> 00:31:05.640
You'll see there
that it says types,

00:31:05.640 --> 00:31:08.160
but there's only
MySQL as a type.

00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:11.310
You can assume that up to this
date, we've only had one type,

00:31:11.310 --> 00:31:13.050
and perhaps we
have another type,

00:31:13.050 --> 00:31:15.750
and that I got told we
didn't announce it yet,

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:17.280
so I pulled it.

00:31:17.280 --> 00:31:21.540
So keep your eyes peeled
for the rest of Next.

00:31:21.540 --> 00:31:24.082
You will find
another type there.

00:31:24.082 --> 00:31:25.290
I figured you guys are smart.

00:31:25.290 --> 00:31:26.165
You'll figure it out.

00:31:26.165 --> 00:31:28.290
So I figured I'd cop to it.

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:32.070
I can't say it, but I can't
tell you to not look for it.

00:31:32.070 --> 00:31:33.990
Our other option
is Cloud Spanner.

00:31:33.990 --> 00:31:37.710
Now, Cloud Spanner is
for structured data,

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:40.440
like traditionally what
you'd store in SQL.

00:31:40.440 --> 00:31:43.110
We advertise it as both
highly available and strongly

00:31:43.110 --> 00:31:45.302
consistent, which the
CAP theorem says--

00:31:45.302 --> 00:31:47.010
if you guys are into
this sort of thing--

00:31:47.010 --> 00:31:49.045
CAP theorem says
that's impossible.

00:31:49.045 --> 00:31:50.670
So what we did is we
found the guy that

00:31:50.670 --> 00:31:54.204
wrote the CAP theorem,
hired him, made him a VP.

00:31:54.204 --> 00:31:56.120
And he came out and was
like, yeah, it's cool.

00:31:56.120 --> 00:31:59.919
You can say that, which is--

00:31:59.919 --> 00:32:01.460
Again, this is a
superficial session.

00:32:01.460 --> 00:32:03.751
It's a very abbreviated
version of the way it happened.

00:32:03.751 --> 00:32:06.510
But long story short, if you
want to dive into this topic

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:09.845
and why we say it's both--

00:32:09.845 --> 00:32:11.970
if that's something you
feel strongly about, please

00:32:11.970 --> 00:32:12.660
see me afterwards.

00:32:12.660 --> 00:32:14.220
I'll be happy to
dig deeper into it.

00:32:14.220 --> 00:32:18.660
But long story short says
we call it highly available

00:32:18.660 --> 00:32:20.261
and strongly consistent.

00:32:20.261 --> 00:32:22.260
It's globally distributed,
and it's horizontally

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:26.970
scalable, which is great
when Cloud SQL starts

00:32:26.970 --> 00:32:28.450
hitting its vertical limit.

00:32:28.450 --> 00:32:31.660
Having a horizontal piece
to this is really helpful.

00:32:31.660 --> 00:32:34.110
Now, it's important to note
that there's a large price

00:32:34.110 --> 00:32:35.631
differential between these two.

00:32:35.631 --> 00:32:38.130
And the reason I say this is
because I just helped a startup

00:32:38.130 --> 00:32:40.710
who had been fooling
around with Spanner,

00:32:40.710 --> 00:32:51.220
not realizing that it's
a BMW, not a Volkswagen.

00:32:51.220 --> 00:32:58.122
And so I would say if you don't
have the need for it, don't--

00:32:58.122 --> 00:33:00.080
if it's not solving the
problem you're having--

00:33:00.080 --> 00:33:01.960
if you're not running into
horizontal scaling problems,

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:02.760
and you're not--

00:33:02.760 --> 00:33:07.200
you don't need
incredibly high latency,

00:33:07.200 --> 00:33:10.710
make sure you read all the fine
print before you spin it up.

00:33:10.710 --> 00:33:12.064
If you need it, it's awesome.

00:33:12.064 --> 00:33:13.730
If you don't need it,
it's a giant bill.

00:33:13.730 --> 00:33:15.882
It's about-- yeah.

00:33:15.882 --> 00:33:17.340
I said I wouldn't
get into pricing,

00:33:17.340 --> 00:33:21.060
but it's more expensive.

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:23.230
The last option is
Cloud Datastore.

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:26.750
Cloud Datastore is a
document-based NoSQL store.

00:33:26.750 --> 00:33:28.340
It's indexable.

00:33:28.340 --> 00:33:31.300
And you can make
it pretty giant.

00:33:31.300 --> 00:33:35.930
We have customers that
store a lot of data in this.

00:33:35.930 --> 00:33:39.290
We also have this thing
called Cloud Bigtable,

00:33:39.290 --> 00:33:41.720
which if you guys follow our
papers, we have Bigtable.

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:44.280
We made a paper about it
but didn't release it.

00:33:44.280 --> 00:33:46.280
And so now there's this
thing called HBase.

00:33:46.280 --> 00:33:48.350
HBase is the open
source version of it.

00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:51.920
And now we need our Cloud
Bigtable, which we released,

00:33:51.920 --> 00:33:52.970
to work with it.

00:33:52.970 --> 00:33:56.110
So it is a columnar,
low-latency database.

00:33:56.110 --> 00:33:58.400
Not a lot of people use
it in an application.

00:33:58.400 --> 00:34:02.180
But I leave it here, because
there are some people that

00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:06.125
see it as a next step for them.

00:34:06.125 --> 00:34:08.750
It's important to note that this
one also-- like Cloud Spanner,

00:34:08.750 --> 00:34:10.969
you really need to need it.

00:34:10.969 --> 00:34:15.530
We say that until you are
storing a terabyte of database

00:34:15.530 --> 00:34:18.500
information,
Bigtable is not going

00:34:18.500 --> 00:34:19.860
to be cost effective for you.

00:34:19.860 --> 00:34:20.735
So keep that in mind.

00:34:24.481 --> 00:34:24.980
What am I--

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:25.489
OK.

00:34:25.489 --> 00:34:27.572
So I talked about files,
I talked about databases,

00:34:27.572 --> 00:34:29.460
and now I'm going to
talk about big data.

00:34:29.460 --> 00:34:33.300
So where do you store big
data on Google Cloud platform?

00:34:35.850 --> 00:34:36.659
In all of these.

00:34:36.659 --> 00:34:40.269
All of these can-- if you've got
two terabytes of data in MySQL,

00:34:40.269 --> 00:34:43.440
it's hard to argue
that's small data.

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:47.290
So any one of these is
our storage for big data.

00:34:47.290 --> 00:34:50.647
What you're really concerned
about with big data is tools.

00:34:50.647 --> 00:34:52.230
So let's talk about
some of the tools.

00:34:52.230 --> 00:34:55.020
Does anybody here
play with BigQuery?

00:34:55.020 --> 00:34:55.909
A couple of people.

00:34:55.909 --> 00:34:57.350
BigQuery is sort of--

00:34:57.350 --> 00:34:59.750
it's magic, especially if
you're not a big data person.

00:34:59.750 --> 00:35:02.550
You're like, how
did you do that?

00:35:02.550 --> 00:35:05.180
You pipe in semi-structured
to structured data.

00:35:05.180 --> 00:35:08.000
You can analyze it very quickly,
and you can use SQL to do it.

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:09.950
And that's its special sauce.

00:35:09.950 --> 00:35:13.012
With most BigQuery big data
jobs, what you're going to do

00:35:13.012 --> 00:35:14.720
is you're going to
build some sort of job

00:35:14.720 --> 00:35:16.136
to analyze your
data, you're going

00:35:16.136 --> 00:35:18.540
to throw as much computing
resources as you can at it,

00:35:18.540 --> 00:35:21.020
and you get the
answer back when it's

00:35:21.020 --> 00:35:22.800
done processing all the data.

00:35:22.800 --> 00:35:24.290
BigQuery is different in that--

00:35:24.290 --> 00:35:26.840
well, that whole piece of
you throw as much computing

00:35:26.840 --> 00:35:29.630
resources as you have at it--

00:35:29.630 --> 00:35:32.524
well, it's us throwing our
computing resources at it.

00:35:32.524 --> 00:35:34.190
And so we keep a whole
bunch of machines

00:35:34.190 --> 00:35:36.770
ready to go to process
BigQuery queries.

00:35:36.770 --> 00:35:42.860
So we can usually see, for
billions of rows, performance

00:35:42.860 --> 00:35:44.522
in under--

00:35:44.522 --> 00:35:45.980
depending on what
you're doing, you

00:35:45.980 --> 00:35:49.460
can get performance of a second
for multi-billion-row queries.

00:35:49.460 --> 00:35:52.610
And in fact, I'm going
to show this off.

00:35:52.610 --> 00:35:54.530
So let's see.

00:35:54.530 --> 00:35:56.390
Here we are-- BigQuery.

00:35:56.390 --> 00:36:02.000
So a friend of mine has,
in their BigQuery storage,

00:36:02.000 --> 00:36:05.030
every Wikipedia
request ever made,

00:36:05.030 --> 00:36:08.120
the logs for all of Wikipedia.

00:36:08.120 --> 00:36:12.530
And so every time someone went
to Tom Hanks' Wikipedia page,

00:36:12.530 --> 00:36:13.700
we flip a counter.

00:36:13.700 --> 00:36:15.110
So we have those logs.

00:36:15.110 --> 00:36:16.140
So that's a lot.

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:17.850
That's a lot of data.

00:36:17.850 --> 00:36:19.750
So I'm going to go--

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:21.200
nope, that's the wrong one.

00:36:21.200 --> 00:36:22.560
Formatted, unformatted.

00:36:22.560 --> 00:36:24.660
Here's a query.

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:27.030
I'm going to edit
it so I can run it.

00:36:27.030 --> 00:36:30.920
You'll see it's SQL, just like
you're mostly used to running.

00:36:30.920 --> 00:36:33.010
And I'm going to run the query.

00:36:33.010 --> 00:36:34.760
And it's going to come
back in one second.

00:36:34.760 --> 00:36:36.676
And you'll see-- that's
because it was cached.

00:36:36.676 --> 00:36:37.460
I was cheating.

00:36:37.460 --> 00:36:41.520
So I'm going uncache
it, just so that we see.

00:36:41.520 --> 00:36:43.250
And I'll run the query.

00:36:43.250 --> 00:36:45.690
And it's going to
take 11 seconds.

00:36:45.690 --> 00:36:46.922
I'm calling it.

00:36:46.922 --> 00:36:49.970
It might take 16, but I
think it's going to take 11.

00:36:49.970 --> 00:36:50.900
Come on.

00:36:50.900 --> 00:36:53.380
A lot of time with me on stage.

00:36:53.380 --> 00:36:56.220
Come on.

00:36:56.220 --> 00:36:58.480
You've been talking to
Compute Engine, haven't you?

00:36:58.480 --> 00:37:00.620
There we go-- 17 seconds.

00:37:00.620 --> 00:37:03.910
So in 17 seconds, we processed
just under a terabyte of data

00:37:03.910 --> 00:37:05.252
to get this number.

00:37:05.252 --> 00:37:07.210
This number at the bottom
is very hard to read.

00:37:07.210 --> 00:37:08.710
I'm going to bump
it up in a second.

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:11.410
But first, what I'm going to do
is actually put commas in it.

00:37:11.410 --> 00:37:15.320
Because otherwise,
I can't read it.

00:37:15.320 --> 00:37:17.110
And I would hope
you guys-- if you

00:37:17.110 --> 00:37:21.091
can parse that number without
commas, I'd be impressed.

00:37:21.091 --> 00:37:21.590
Here we go.

00:37:21.590 --> 00:37:22.840
We're going to run this query.

00:37:27.230 --> 00:37:27.765
No, no.

00:37:27.765 --> 00:37:28.640
That's the wrong one.

00:37:28.640 --> 00:37:29.370
Yes, cancel.

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:29.870
Sorry.

00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:33.260
There we go.

00:37:35.972 --> 00:37:36.880
Sorry.

00:37:36.880 --> 00:37:37.800
There we go.

00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:38.300
All right.

00:37:38.300 --> 00:37:39.514
Now I'll run the query.

00:37:39.514 --> 00:37:41.930
This will take 20 seconds, and
it will actually format it.

00:37:41.930 --> 00:37:43.340
But long story short--

00:37:43.340 --> 00:37:46.190
and I'll show the explanation
of all of this when it's done.

00:37:46.190 --> 00:37:48.170
We're going to go
through another--

00:37:48.170 --> 00:37:49.190
what was it--

00:37:49.190 --> 00:37:51.660
0.9 terabytes of data.

00:37:51.660 --> 00:37:54.260
And it's going to go about
20 seconds this time.

00:37:54.260 --> 00:37:56.150
I'm going to be pessimistic.

00:37:56.150 --> 00:37:58.590
We're not caching, because
I turned off the caching.

00:37:58.590 --> 00:37:59.400
So there we go--

00:37:59.400 --> 00:38:01.590
19 seconds, just
to make me wrong.

00:38:01.590 --> 00:38:03.770
So that number,
if you can see it,

00:38:03.770 --> 00:38:07.680
is we calculated-- we
had it up 1 trillion,

00:38:07.680 --> 00:38:14.970
190,543,847,386
requests to Wikipedia

00:38:14.970 --> 00:38:19.742
for two years looking
at the query there.

00:38:19.742 --> 00:38:20.700
That's kind of awesome.

00:38:20.700 --> 00:38:23.035
And if we look at what
it did under the covers--

00:38:23.035 --> 00:38:25.590
I've got to shrink
down my screen--

00:38:25.590 --> 00:38:27.200
we can actually
see an explanation.

00:38:27.200 --> 00:38:30.060
And we can see what it
did is that it started out

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:35.430
with 130 billion rows of
data from all the Wikipedia

00:38:35.430 --> 00:38:36.109
entries it has.

00:38:36.109 --> 00:38:36.900
You see here it is.

00:38:36.900 --> 00:38:39.450
It's all the requests of
all the tables it called.

00:38:39.450 --> 00:38:42.000
And then it shrunk
it down to 65,000.

00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:43.590
So it did a MapReduce on it.

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:46.320
And then we reduced it again
to the one answer of that.

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:49.080
We did all of that
in 20 seconds.

00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:50.920
We counted up to 1
trillion processing

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:52.950
130 billion rows of data.

00:38:52.950 --> 00:38:55.510
Is that OK?

00:38:55.510 --> 00:38:56.310
Yeah?

00:38:56.310 --> 00:38:58.800
All right.

00:38:58.800 --> 00:39:01.890
Tough crowd.

00:39:01.890 --> 00:39:02.710
Lunch is looming.

00:39:02.710 --> 00:39:03.210
I know.

00:39:03.210 --> 00:39:05.970
So big data, not storage--

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:07.902
PubSub, publisher
subscriber model--

00:39:07.902 --> 00:39:09.360
basically allows
you to communicate

00:39:09.360 --> 00:39:11.280
between multiple
pieces of our platform.

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:13.140
Use it a lot when
using the database

00:39:13.140 --> 00:39:15.400
tools I'm about to talk about.

00:39:15.400 --> 00:39:19.220
We can easily handle a
million messages per second,

00:39:19.220 --> 00:39:21.950
meaning if you're going to do
less than a million messages

00:39:21.950 --> 00:39:23.960
per second, you don't
really have to talk to us.

00:39:23.960 --> 00:39:25.460
If you're doing
more than a million,

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:27.620
we'd just like a heads up.

00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:30.890
So keep that in mind if
you're starting to move over

00:39:30.890 --> 00:39:33.320
and do lots of messaging
on our platform.

00:39:33.320 --> 00:39:37.210
Data flow-- anybody
here use Apache Beam?

00:39:37.210 --> 00:39:37.990
No.

00:39:37.990 --> 00:39:38.690
I saw one.

00:39:38.690 --> 00:39:40.450
Apache Beam, but managed.

00:39:40.450 --> 00:39:42.730
So does data
transformation for you.

00:39:42.730 --> 00:39:45.550
You line up your workload,
transfer the data

00:39:45.550 --> 00:39:47.320
as it goes into an
analysis system,

00:39:47.320 --> 00:39:49.480
and you can repeat it
multiple, multiple times.

00:39:49.480 --> 00:39:52.420
And then Dataproc, which
is Managed Spark and Hadoop

00:39:52.420 --> 00:39:54.490
with the added benefit
of allowing auto scaling.

00:39:54.490 --> 00:39:56.800
So it makes building
out your jobs

00:39:56.800 --> 00:40:01.810
and throwing more resources
at it much more easy.

00:40:01.810 --> 00:40:04.380
Important to note, just
as a side note, that

00:40:04.380 --> 00:40:06.660
between Cloud Storage
and Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:40:06.660 --> 00:40:10.210
you can interconnect any one
of the rest of our products.

00:40:10.210 --> 00:40:12.150
So if you're building
an app on App Engine,

00:40:12.150 --> 00:40:14.430
and you want to send it to
our machine learning APIs--

00:40:14.430 --> 00:40:17.640
like, say, the Vision
API deals with images--

00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:20.910
you can directly send
the image to the API.

00:40:20.910 --> 00:40:22.590
But you have to
base 64 encode it,

00:40:22.590 --> 00:40:26.430
and it's not a very
efficient process.

00:40:26.430 --> 00:40:29.670
Or you can store
it in Cloud Storage

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:32.760
and then tell Cloud Vision API,
hey, I've got a file for you,

00:40:32.760 --> 00:40:35.100
and it will handle it.

00:40:35.100 --> 00:40:37.230
So almost any one
of these tools can

00:40:37.230 --> 00:40:39.794
be connected to any of the
other tools through these two.

00:40:39.794 --> 00:40:41.460
So important to keep
in mind that as you

00:40:41.460 --> 00:40:44.580
start to solve problems, like
how do I build out systems,

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:47.030
this is one way to do it.

00:40:47.030 --> 00:40:49.700
What if you need
something we don't offer?

00:40:49.700 --> 00:40:51.800
We have non-managed
storage solutions

00:40:51.800 --> 00:40:53.330
that you can build by hand.

00:40:53.330 --> 00:40:56.270
Or you can go to Cloud Launcher
and get Cassandra, Reds, Mongo,

00:40:56.270 --> 00:40:57.770
CouchDB or others.

00:40:57.770 --> 00:40:59.960
Did not go
alphabetically this time.

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:02.930
I don't know what my
order of preference was.

00:41:02.930 --> 00:41:04.109
So that is storage.

00:41:04.109 --> 00:41:05.900
Hopefully, that gives
you a good indication

00:41:05.900 --> 00:41:08.024
of where you're going to
find the storage you need.

00:41:08.024 --> 00:41:10.310
We're coming in the
homestretch, and we're

00:41:10.310 --> 00:41:12.710
going to administrations.

00:41:12.710 --> 00:41:16.100
So administration is usually
worried about three things--

00:41:16.100 --> 00:41:18.650
reporting, security,
and configuration.

00:41:18.650 --> 00:41:22.100
If you've noticed, I like
to make three things slides.

00:41:22.100 --> 00:41:24.790
You'll see that theme
throughout here.

00:41:24.790 --> 00:41:27.740
Reporting-- Stackdriver.

00:41:27.740 --> 00:41:29.995
Now, you'll notice
we have-- we'll

00:41:29.995 --> 00:41:32.480
call a whole bunch of
tools we have Stackdriver,

00:41:32.480 --> 00:41:34.910
which is weird, because
it's Google Cloud Platform.

00:41:34.910 --> 00:41:36.530
Why is it Stackdriver?

00:41:36.530 --> 00:41:39.084
We do that so that
you can wrap--

00:41:39.084 --> 00:41:41.000
so it's easy to wrap
your mind around the idea

00:41:41.000 --> 00:41:46.800
that Stackdriver is actually a
hybrid or cross-cloud solution.

00:41:46.800 --> 00:41:49.530
So you can run it on us, and
we set it up automatically

00:41:49.530 --> 00:41:50.250
for you.

00:41:50.250 --> 00:41:55.060
But on AWS, most of our
products for reporting

00:41:55.060 --> 00:41:59.400
have a one-click or a very
easy solution to get it running

00:41:59.400 --> 00:42:02.610
and spin it up on Amazon.

00:42:02.610 --> 00:42:04.980
And then on prem, because
all of this has an API,

00:42:04.980 --> 00:42:05.940
you can extend--

00:42:05.940 --> 00:42:08.730
between the API that
we have and the SDK,

00:42:08.730 --> 00:42:11.280
you can write your own tools
that will talk to these things.

00:42:11.280 --> 00:42:13.200
So we've got logging.

00:42:13.200 --> 00:42:14.380
We've got monitoring.

00:42:14.380 --> 00:42:17.470
You can build up alerts based
on things you're used to,

00:42:17.470 --> 00:42:21.310
uptime checks, built-in
metrics, and so forth.

00:42:21.310 --> 00:42:25.690
For security, this is important
for a lot of companies.

00:42:25.690 --> 00:42:27.610
We now have Cloud IM.

00:42:27.610 --> 00:42:29.124
So I'm not going to--

00:42:29.124 --> 00:42:30.790
it could be its own
talk too, so I'm not

00:42:30.790 --> 00:42:32.620
going dig too deep into it.

00:42:32.620 --> 00:42:35.080
But just find Cloud IAM.

00:42:35.080 --> 00:42:37.630
You can create
accounts that you then

00:42:37.630 --> 00:42:39.700
can assign to roles
and permissions,

00:42:39.700 --> 00:42:44.400
then use to lock down various
pieces of your account--

00:42:44.400 --> 00:42:48.370
sorry, of your infrastructure.

00:42:48.370 --> 00:42:49.810
So finally,
configuration-- you're

00:42:49.810 --> 00:42:51.393
going to do most of
your configuration

00:42:51.393 --> 00:42:53.170
probably command
line, probably using

00:42:53.170 --> 00:42:54.890
the GCloud or the GSutil tool.

00:42:54.890 --> 00:42:58.400
GCloud will do
almost everything.

00:42:58.400 --> 00:43:02.020
But if you actually want to deal
with files on cloud storage,

00:43:02.020 --> 00:43:05.575
you're going to want to
use GSutil, because it's--

00:43:05.575 --> 00:43:08.170
GCloud is for management
of the systems.

00:43:08.170 --> 00:43:11.990
GSutil is actually for reading
and writing from cloud storage.

00:43:11.990 --> 00:43:14.716
We also have an API with
an SDK for most languages

00:43:14.716 --> 00:43:16.840
that gives you REST endpoints
that are functionally

00:43:16.840 --> 00:43:17.800
equivalent to GCloud.

00:43:17.800 --> 00:43:20.133
So you can build your own
solutions in whatever language

00:43:20.133 --> 00:43:24.220
you want and also call
them similar to GCloud.

00:43:24.220 --> 00:43:26.710
And then finally,
Deployment Manager--

00:43:26.710 --> 00:43:30.940
if you like writing massive
YAML files, as we all do,

00:43:30.940 --> 00:43:35.285
you can write out your
entire environment in YAML

00:43:35.285 --> 00:43:37.900
and then reproduce it,
either on your own project

00:43:37.900 --> 00:43:41.110
or on new projects, with
network configurations,

00:43:41.110 --> 00:43:43.960
with what servers
are set up with VMs,

00:43:43.960 --> 00:43:46.930
what Kubernetes setup you have.

00:43:46.930 --> 00:43:50.020
And this is actually how
Cloud Launcher works.

00:43:50.020 --> 00:43:55.080
So we use it internally,
and it is available to you.

00:43:55.080 --> 00:43:58.190
Moving on to the development.

00:43:58.190 --> 00:44:00.620
Developers in general,
we think about coding.

00:44:00.620 --> 00:44:02.270
We think about
reporting sometimes.

00:44:02.270 --> 00:44:04.760
And we like using
services, because then we

00:44:04.760 --> 00:44:07.370
don't have to write it.

00:44:07.370 --> 00:44:09.015
So development--
we have the SDK.

00:44:09.015 --> 00:44:11.390
Important to note that most
of the things you want to do,

00:44:11.390 --> 00:44:13.140
you can do through GCoud.

00:44:13.140 --> 00:44:15.770
If you're using App
Engine, you also

00:44:15.770 --> 00:44:18.050
want to make sure
you have App Config.

00:44:18.050 --> 00:44:21.020
Because the PMs
tell me that GCloud

00:44:21.020 --> 00:44:23.060
can do everything that
App Config can do,

00:44:23.060 --> 00:44:24.770
but they're lying.

00:44:24.770 --> 00:44:25.840
Or they're wrong.

00:44:25.840 --> 00:44:28.680
But they're lying.

00:44:28.680 --> 00:44:33.650
Plugins-- for any ID you have,
we can plug into most of them

00:44:33.650 --> 00:44:37.220
with tools that help
basic code intelligence

00:44:37.220 --> 00:44:39.410
of our commands and our APIs.

00:44:39.410 --> 00:44:41.120
And then others--
I clearly ran out

00:44:41.120 --> 00:44:44.450
of my three-tiered icon system.

00:44:44.450 --> 00:44:45.794
We have Cloud Source Repository.

00:44:45.794 --> 00:44:48.085
So we have managed git that
is locked with our security

00:44:48.085 --> 00:44:50.360
that you set up
through Cloud IAM.

00:44:50.360 --> 00:44:52.460
And it works with one
of our other tools

00:44:52.460 --> 00:44:55.180
that I'll talk about in a
second, Cloud Debugging.

00:44:55.180 --> 00:45:00.040
So moving on to reporting,
we have Stackdriver Trace,

00:45:00.040 --> 00:45:02.230
which is installed
automatically on App Engine

00:45:02.230 --> 00:45:03.656
and on load balancers.

00:45:03.656 --> 00:45:05.530
You can enable it through
an SDK and a couple

00:45:05.530 --> 00:45:07.209
of other environments.

00:45:07.209 --> 00:45:09.250
And I'll just show it,
because it's kind of cool.

00:45:09.250 --> 00:45:11.791
If you guys have seen any of
the showcase demos, which are up

00:45:11.791 --> 00:45:14.950
on the third floor, there's
one with a giant pi symbol

00:45:14.950 --> 00:45:17.950
where you spin up and build pi.

00:45:17.950 --> 00:45:19.750
You crunch 100
million digits of pi

00:45:19.750 --> 00:45:21.380
in a very short period of time.

00:45:21.380 --> 00:45:25.062
So that application I was
having some trouble with,

00:45:25.062 --> 00:45:26.770
and I was looking into
what was going on.

00:45:26.770 --> 00:45:30.160
And you see down here, I
get pretty basic information

00:45:30.160 --> 00:45:31.330
about what's going on.

00:45:31.330 --> 00:45:33.430
The whole request
took 92 milliseconds.

00:45:33.430 --> 00:45:35.050
I made a call to the
identity service,

00:45:35.050 --> 00:45:37.970
and then I went and made
a call to another API.

00:45:37.970 --> 00:45:40.540
I can then click on that
button to show the logs

00:45:40.540 --> 00:45:42.450
to see what was going
on at the same time.

00:45:42.450 --> 00:45:46.710
And I can see distribution for
how long that same request took

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:48.520
the many different
times it was taken.

00:45:48.520 --> 00:45:51.661
And I see-- oh, there was an
anomaly versus, oh, there's

00:45:51.661 --> 00:45:53.410
a whole bunch that are
taking a long time.

00:45:53.410 --> 00:45:54.910
There's a problem.

00:45:54.910 --> 00:45:56.530
So that is trace.

00:45:56.530 --> 00:46:00.200
It's really, really great when
you start digging into it.

00:46:00.200 --> 00:46:02.680
So also, error reporting
for aggregating either

00:46:02.680 --> 00:46:06.590
log data or stuff that you
sent directly to the API.

00:46:06.590 --> 00:46:09.479
It is also really helpful
to see, is this a--

00:46:09.479 --> 00:46:11.770
again, is this an anomalous
error, or is this something

00:46:11.770 --> 00:46:14.720
that I now need to take care of.

00:46:14.720 --> 00:46:17.890
Debugging-- again, if
you use cloud repos--

00:46:17.890 --> 00:46:20.750
and this, you can actually
step debug into your code

00:46:20.750 --> 00:46:23.920
and then go directly into your
code on Cloud Repo for stuff

00:46:23.920 --> 00:46:28.360
running in production if you
are brave enough and bold enough

00:46:28.360 --> 00:46:29.792
to do that.

00:46:29.792 --> 00:46:31.750
It's available in App
Engine and Compute Engine

00:46:31.750 --> 00:46:35.310
in a couple of
different run times.

00:46:35.310 --> 00:46:38.250
So services-- we have
all of these services,

00:46:38.250 --> 00:46:39.990
and not just us.

00:46:39.990 --> 00:46:41.400
Cloud has a bunch.

00:46:41.400 --> 00:46:45.919
We also have Ads and GSuite
and YouTube and other things.

00:46:45.919 --> 00:46:47.460
The point I want to
get across to you

00:46:47.460 --> 00:46:49.166
here is that most
of the time, you

00:46:49.166 --> 00:46:50.790
need to set up some
sort of certificate

00:46:50.790 --> 00:46:54.350
or some sort of security between
these services and your cloud

00:46:54.350 --> 00:46:55.050
project.

00:46:55.050 --> 00:46:58.740
However, we have this feature
called Default Credentials,

00:46:58.740 --> 00:47:02.890
and they work on App Engine and
Compute Engine out of the box.

00:47:02.890 --> 00:47:04.370
And it's beautiful.

00:47:04.370 --> 00:47:06.960
Because all you have to do
is instead of setting up

00:47:06.960 --> 00:47:09.510
a whole set of certificates,
and putting them in your code,

00:47:09.510 --> 00:47:12.600
and storing them, and worrying
about managing that and not

00:47:12.600 --> 00:47:15.090
accidentally adding
them to your git repo,

00:47:15.090 --> 00:47:17.190
you can just say, give me
the default credentials

00:47:17.190 --> 00:47:18.300
to call the service.

00:47:18.300 --> 00:47:20.070
And that's all you have to do.

00:47:20.070 --> 00:47:22.800
So it makes dealing with
these services a lot easier.

00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:25.710
So it's something
to keep in mind.

00:47:25.710 --> 00:47:27.570
I'm going to finish
with machine learning.

00:47:27.570 --> 00:47:30.970
I am running OK on time.

00:47:30.970 --> 00:47:34.350
And so machine learning tends
to be about three things--

00:47:34.350 --> 00:47:38.730
using models, creating
models, or extending models.

00:47:38.730 --> 00:47:41.340
So we have a lot of
models that you can use--

00:47:41.340 --> 00:47:45.120
Vision API for images,
Natural Language API for text,

00:47:45.120 --> 00:47:46.751
Speech API, Translation API.

00:47:46.751 --> 00:47:47.250
Use them.

00:47:47.250 --> 00:47:48.090
They're awesome.

00:47:48.090 --> 00:47:50.850
If you want to
take the next step

00:47:50.850 --> 00:47:53.070
and not use the model
but train models,

00:47:53.070 --> 00:47:56.400
you want to use Cloud ML engine.

00:47:56.400 --> 00:47:59.100
It is managed TensorFlow,
which you should be asking--

00:47:59.100 --> 00:48:00.940
what flow?

00:48:00.940 --> 00:48:03.050
TensorFlow is an open
source ML library

00:48:03.050 --> 00:48:06.260
that allows you to create
portable models that you

00:48:06.260 --> 00:48:09.440
can train in one place and
also train in other places.

00:48:09.440 --> 00:48:12.440
The idea here is that you can
train your model with varying

00:48:12.440 --> 00:48:14.122
levels of GPU and CPU power.

00:48:14.122 --> 00:48:16.580
And it's OK, because you can
move them from place to place.

00:48:16.580 --> 00:48:22.310
So when we get back to machine
learning, Cloud ML Engine--

00:48:22.310 --> 00:48:25.270
we just changed the name, so
I apologize for bobbling it--

00:48:25.270 --> 00:48:26.894
it's managed
TensorFlow, which does

00:48:26.894 --> 00:48:28.810
a whole lot of self-tuning,
and it's portable.

00:48:28.810 --> 00:48:31.390
So you can create a
model on your laptop

00:48:31.390 --> 00:48:33.910
and move it to
Cloud ML and have it

00:48:33.910 --> 00:48:36.820
run in Cloud ML with
much more resources

00:48:36.820 --> 00:48:38.890
that then get self-tuned.

00:48:38.890 --> 00:48:41.710
So that's it.

00:48:41.710 --> 00:48:44.740
So conclusions-- so
hopefully, after all of this,

00:48:44.740 --> 00:48:48.820
we're now putting the table
back up that we flipped over

00:48:48.820 --> 00:48:51.160
when we saw this.

00:48:51.160 --> 00:48:54.050
There is lots on
Google Cloud platform.

00:48:54.050 --> 00:48:55.910
And we totally acknowledge this.

00:48:55.910 --> 00:48:57.760
And we are working on it.

00:48:57.760 --> 00:48:59.650
We're making this bigger.

00:48:59.650 --> 00:49:01.330
There are 50 or so--

00:49:01.330 --> 00:49:03.040
50-something icons up here.

00:49:03.040 --> 00:49:05.354
I talked about 43 individual
products in this hour.

00:49:05.354 --> 00:49:07.270
Some of it was auctioneer
call, and some of it

00:49:07.270 --> 00:49:10.300
was designed to help you make
strategic decisions around it.

00:49:10.300 --> 00:49:13.690
I hope at the end
of this, you now

00:49:13.690 --> 00:49:15.220
have a better idea
of where you need

00:49:15.220 --> 00:49:17.200
to go in order to
find the answers

00:49:17.200 --> 00:49:20.020
and solve the problems
you want to solve.

00:49:20.020 --> 00:49:21.190
I am around.

00:49:21.190 --> 00:49:23.270
My Twitter handle has
been on all the slides.

00:49:23.270 --> 00:49:24.770
So if you need to
ask me a question,

00:49:24.770 --> 00:49:26.200
feel free to ask me a question.

00:49:26.200 --> 00:49:28.430
You will often also
catch me out smoking,

00:49:28.430 --> 00:49:30.820
so feel free to do that, too.

00:49:30.820 --> 00:49:32.980
And with that, I'm going
